u trying to figure out how they  look like as a family and as you cast  certain actors are you making  adjustments to what they're going to say  and what they're going to do in the  screenplay yeah um i i met and  auditioned i think more actors on this  movie than any i'd done you know for  instance in the book and in the original  versions of the script uh it was a pure  jewish family so for j when it was jane  for instance we made that dad was jewish  and mom wasn't um when we cast adam  driver who plays philip the youngest  brother he had such a kind of odd and  fantastically unique quality his rhythms  are really kind of off and so jonathan  and i would rework that role so that it  felt more organic to his voice and  similarly bateman who's the master of  dry  arch wit we brought it closer to his  voice and the kind of voices the  characters revealed themselves and then  tailored themselves or rather we  tailored the dialogue around the voice  of the given actor so you're working  with the novelist and the screenwriter  and jonathan had said when he sold the  book that he wanted to be the  screenwriter what were the most intense  conversations you had about the  adaptation  interestingly it's the opposite of what  people assume the general fear is that  if the novelist writes the screenplay  they'll be too faithful to kind of  rigidly adherent to the novel  what jonathan had done is i think  jonathan  assumed that for a movie like this to  get made it needed to be a broader  comedy than the book was and so when i  decided to make the movie the draft at  that point was quite a bit more broad it  had like the siblings sneaking into  dad's sporting goods store and falling  off the roof and it was a broader kind  of thing so our process was i went back  to my copy of the novel where i had  underlined specific lines that i found  just magnificent because i think  jonathan is a  really magnificent writer and our work  together was me saying to jonathan  you need to  you need to be more loyal to your book  you need to not be shy  about honoring  your prose in screenplay form and so we  brought a lot more of the dramatic  content from the novel into the  screenplay and movie what was it like  working with actors who are probably  going to be as inclined to improvise as  they are to to read a script  well i had done a lot of improv in drama  you know coming home was  very much improv but improv comedy is  something totally different  and i learned very fast that i can't do  it  and to be completely in awe of those who  can and here are these people who are  the best and uh  it was just and i just sort of sat there  with my chin on my chest  i'm wondering in your initial  conversations about how you wanted to  play this character and sean what you  wanted out of it what were the kinds of  things you talked about and what were  the commonalities of your conversation i  don't remember  it's what one thing tell me well well  i don't know whether any of this seeped  in um  first you know we just did a little kind  of just to connect more jane and i had  dinner with tina in new york and i  brought my my jewish stepmom down from  montreal just because i felt like there  was a warmth and a kind of i don't know  there's a word like hamisha it's just  like firmpth  yes firm i what is i don't actually know  means firm and warm okay so that yeah um  we talked about it a bit but the truth  is i feel like jane brought  so many of the right instincts because  as you've said to me jane uh the kind of  hillary's sexual candor hillary's kind  of sharing over sharing i'm sure you you  say it's it's often been to the  embarrassment of your own children so  that's something you related to and  totally yeah  my son takes me to lunch i think  probably once every three months he's an  actor troy garrity and he'll take me to  lunch where my daughter-in-law and well  mom  it's come to my attention that last week  you said or did such and such and i  think maybe  you need to cool it  or you're what you're wearing right now  mom  trying too hard  you know and it's cool when that happens  but anyway so i do identify with her and  and so so much i feel like we didn't  really have to build a character from  scratch because the reason jane got the  part is jane so much of what she brought  her warmth her firm her  transparency  her bluntness um that's what hillary  needed to be and also hillary needed to  be a kind of estimable matriarch she  needed to be a major  presence in this family and jane fonda  brings that into the room with her thank  you  i'm curious as you're not only shooting  the film but going through editorial  where did you see the movie going and  the tone of it ended up being one of the  bigger struggles that you had and in  which way were you kind of scaling back  um  the truth is the you know like any time  you direct a movie you follow your  instinct on this stuff and um i'm aware  that the movie is both you know very  profane in points and it has  it's got a fair bit of swearing and it  has some sexual jokes and boob stuff and  all that but and that it also  asks the audience to stick around  and invest in what will be hopefully  poignant moments um and you know if the  movie succeeds hopefully we get those  laughs but we also get those moments  that are moving you know when  when philip and  you know judd touch foreheads when  judd's leaving hopefully we've earned  the right for a moment like that too um  so much of it is intuitive i don't have  a a great answer to that so sean what is  it like when you're working with actors  who have such radically distinct styles  some are going to go off books some are  going to riff jane is probably going to  say her dialogue as it's written what do  you do on on the day well i think this  is true in any movie  i i felt it the most kind of acutely on  this movie because it was  7 to 11 of us in a room in a real house  for 25 days straight so that's a real  house we were in that living room and um  so i i was very conscious of what what  happens on every movie as a director i  don't know how it is for other  filmmakers in in this room but  what i love about the job is you have to  tailor your language  to each actor anyway that's the job you  don't give a note from behind the camera  a i don't ever give a note publicly like  that and then i really try and customize  you know how i speak to my actors so  with jane for instance i'll just i would  go and i'd sit in the shiva chair next  to her and i would make suggestions  about maybe you know how we might take  the scene a certain way whereas with  with someone like um  you know with someone like adam driver i  don't want him in his head  that guy is is great because he's pure  instinct so with adam i said as little  as possible and  i i kind of let him surprise himself  bateman and tina are brilliant  improvisers but thankfully they're both  also producers and filmmakers in their  own right so they know what a scene  needs to accomplish so you know one  example for instance is the scene where  tina's trying to get bateman to tell  everyone about his divorce and she's  pulling his hair that scene is fifty  percent written fifty percent improvised  and yet um you know that was a matter of  i went to ja to tina and i said make him  tell do anything you have to make him  tell and then i went over to bateman and  i said shut her up  shut her up don't reveal your secret and  then we rolled and i said action so it's  always great of course when you have two  actors playing radically opposing  objectives

